analysis:
For each run \(r\), Cauchy–Schwarz gives \[{\rm MAE}_r=\frac1n\sum_i|e_{ri}|
 \leq\sqrt{\frac1n\sum_i e_{ri}^{2}}
 =\sqrt{{\rm MSE}_r}.\] Averaging over the three runs and applying concavity of the square root yields \[\overline{\rm MAE}\leq\sqrt{\overline{\rm MSE}}
 =\sqrt{2.624}\simeq1.61988,\] far below the reported \(2.624\). Rounding cannot bridge the gap: even the upper rounded MSE \(2.6245\) permits only \(1.62004\), whereas the lower rounded MAE is \(2.6235\). Both entries were visually verified in the original PDF. The identical mean and standard error suggest a copied cell, but that cause is not established.

Delete \(a\), leaving no clients, and set \(x^{2}=0\). The empty-instance optimum is zero and this is a valid exact solution. The fractional movement is one. The old ball has mass zero and is dropped, so \(\Delta N_1=1,\Delta N_2=0,\Phi^{2}=0\). the displayed formula would require a universal constant to bound \[
1+\eta\log_2\frac{2}{\gamma s}
\le O(\log2)\cdot1.\] The left side is unbounded as \(s\downarrow0\), whereas the right side is constant. Thus the actual inductive inequality, not just its terminal estimate, is false. Empty states are explicitly used in the paper’s initialization and lower-bound phases; excluding them would change the stated model.

