analysis:
The distinction is material: lists of \((p,L(p))\) compare first by the binary path \(p\). An independent enumeration of the 4,862 valid paths to \((10,9)\) supplies a finite witness. Encode east by 1 and north by 0, and set \[\begin{aligned}
 p&=1101010101011001100,\\
 q&=1101010101011010010.\end{aligned}\] Both remain strictly below \(y=x\) at intermediate integer points and hence below the endpoint diagonal \(y=9x/10\). Each lies in a singleton \((D,q)\) group in the independent enumeration. They satisfy \(p<q\) lexicographically, but \[\begin{aligned}
 L(p)^{2}&=24668154583661/2459580573025,\\
 L(q)^{2}&=499765652882717/55353495840049,\end{aligned}\] so \(L(p)\approx3.16692518>L(q)\approx3.00476613\). Singleton groups make this relative-order reversal independent of the enumeration order of paths within a group.

For every inversion coordinate \(Y_{ij}=\mathbf1\{w_i>w_j\}\), exactly half of all permutations have value 1: exchanging the values at positions \(i,j\) is a fixed-point-free pairing that reverses the comparison. Let \(a\in(0,1)\) be the training fraction, \(p\) its mean on this coordinate and \(q\) the test mean. Exhaustive, disjoint coverage gives \[ap+(1-a)q=\tfrac12.\] The mean predictor’s test error on this coordinate is consequently \[
 q(1-p)^{2}+(1-q)p^{2}
 =\frac14+\frac{1+a}{1-a}\left(p-\frac12\right)^{2}\ge\frac14.\] Averaging coordinates preserves the bound. No assumption of random splitting or large sample size is needed. The current loader, `load_datasets.py` lines 469–481, constructs all pairwise inversion bits, and the RSK generation notebook partitions all permutations using the same shuffled indices for tableaux and targets. It corroborates the stated setup but does not establish the historical implementation. Independent rational checks verify (the displayed formula).

Let a rare input have probability \(0.01\), let \(a=10^{-6}\), and let \(F\) contain all binary predictors whose rare-point positive probability lies in \([a,2a]\), with probability \(1/2\) elsewhere. This class is convex and uniformly positive. Let \(g=w=q\) use \(a\) at the rare input, and student \(p\) use \(2a\). The minimizing projection is \(q\), and \(p\in\operatorname{co}^kF\) for any \(k\). Calculation gives \[\rho_1=10001.98,\quad \rho_2=1.99000002,\quad
 \mathbb E D_{\rm KL}(p\|q)=3.8629486112\,10^{-9}.\] The intermediate expression in the printed proof is \[\mathbb E\frac{\sqrt{2D_{\rm KL}(p(X)\|q(X))}}
                   {\min_i(p_i(X),q_i(X))}
   =8.7897083128.\] Take \(k=10^{18}\) and the optimization tolerance in that proof to be \(\varepsilon=\mathbb E D_{\rm KL}(p\|q)\); the student meets that tolerance exactly. Its proposed upper bound \[\rho\sqrt{\frac{c\rho}{4k}+\varepsilon},
 \qquad \rho=\max_i\rho_i,\quad c=2,\] is only \(0.6216496999\). Even replacing the tolerance term by twice its value does not fix the discrepancy. This is a false intermediate inequality, not just an omitted justification.

Every tail point originally belongs to \(b\), because \(u\ge3/4>1/2\). The boundary between \(a'\) and \(b\) is \[u=\frac12+\epsilon v-\frac{\epsilon^{2}}{2}.\] For \(v\ge1/\epsilon\), this boundary is larger than 1, whereas the entire tail has \(u<1\). Thus all tail area above \(1/\epsilon\) moves into the combined \(a,a'\) cell. Adding \(a'\) cannot remove any old \(a\) cell from that combined cell. Consequently the increase \(q\) in the merged weight satisfies \[
 q\ge \frac1V\int_{1/\epsilon}^{\infty}\frac{dv}{v(\log v)^{2}}
   =\frac1{V\log(1/\epsilon)}.\] This is not \(O(\epsilon)\).

claim:
For \(r=(1,0,0)\), direct evaluation of the ordered-pair objective gives, with \(w_0=0.005\), \[f_2(r)-f_2(0)
 =w_0(1-w_0)\log\cosh(1/2)+\frac{w_0}{2}
 =0.00309756967211743\ldots.\] Even the correctly halved strong-convexity lower bound using the old modulus would be \((1\cdot0.5/2)\|r-r_2\|_2^{2}=0.25\), which fails. The new-weight lower bound \(0.0025\) is compatible with the calculation. Hence the old modulus cannot justify the displayed implication. This witness has a bounded regular domain and strictly positive weights; it is separate from Problem 1.

analysis:
There is a stronger local argument that avoids a minimum-weight bound. For two objectives sharing positive weights and \(\lambda\), divide their first-order conditions by each coordinate’s positive weight. For reward difference \(h=\widehat r-r_2\), subtraction gives \[\lambda h_i+\sum_j w_j\{\sigma(\widehat r_i-\widehat r_j)
                  -\sigma((r_2)_i-(r_2)_j)\}
       =\sum_jw_j(p'_{ij}-p^{(2)}_{ij}).\] At a coordinate maximizing \(h_i\), every sigmoid difference on the left is nonnegative. The minimum coordinate gives the reverse bound. Therefore \[
 \|\widehat r-r_2\|_\infty
 \le\frac{\max_{i,j}|p'_{ij}-p^{(2)}_{ij}|}{\lambda}
 \le\frac{K\epsilon}{4\lambda},\] using the sigmoid derivative bound \(1/4\) and the original Lipschitz assumption. This holds uniformly despite shrinking positive clone weights and was independently challenged.

For Equation (1), differentiation instead gives \[0=\lambda r_x+\sum_y\{\sigma(r_x-r_y)-p_{xy}\},\] and hence \[\operatorname{AWR}_D(x)=\frac{\lambda}{m}r_x+
                  \widehat{\operatorname{AWR}}(x).\] The one-sentence proof in Appendix F substitutes weights 1 into the weighted identity, but does not divide the regularizer term when converting sums into averages. With uniform \(w_x=1/m\), Equation (2) is \[f_w(r)=\frac1{m^{2}}L_M(r)+\frac{\lambda}{2m}\sum_xr_x^{2}
       =\frac1{m^{2}}F_{m\lambda}(r),\] so it matches the standard optimizer at regularization \(m\lambda\), not \(\lambda\).

The invalid implication on p. 16 is \[\gamma\le\frac1{2(1+\sigma_1^{2})}
 \ \Longrightarrow\
 \frac12-\frac{L(1+\sigma_1^{2})\gamma}{2}\ge\frac14.\] It needs \(\gamma\le1/[2L(1+\sigma_1^{2})]\). This is not merely a harmless displayed typo because the admissible schedule and theorem conclusion demonstrably fail.

paragraphs:
Verified repair scope. The corrected restriction repairs that descent step. For the no-shift, no-DP setting, put \(k=(G-c)_+\). Smoothness, \(\|\mathbb E\,\mathrm{clip}(g)-F\|\le k\), and Young’s inequality give \[\sum_t\gamma_{t+1}\mathbb E\|F(\theta_t)\|^{2}
 \le4\Delta_0+2L\sigma_0^{2}\sum_t\gamma_{t+1}^{2}
       +2k^{2}\sum_t\gamma_{t+1}.\] This independently proves a restricted corrected bound. The full printed constants for distribution shifts and DP noise are not certified by this repair.

analysis:
Conditions (i) and (iii), confirmed in original page images, require \[\gamma_t=\frac{a_0}{a_1+t},\qquad
 \frac{\gamma_t^{2}}{\gamma_{t+1}^{2}}\le1+\bar b\gamma_{t+1}^2.\] For \(a_0>0\) and \(x=a_1+t\), this implies \[\bar b\ge\frac{(2x+1)(x+1)^{2}}{a_0^2x^{2}}
 \sim \frac{2x}{a_0^{2}}\longrightarrow\infty.\] No finite constant \(\bar b\) satisfies the infinite-horizon conditions. The printed implication is therefore vacuous for the schedule used to conclude \(O(1/t)\) mean-square convergence. It does not prove that DiceSGD cannot converge.

A3 bounds gradients over the entire product \(\mathcal X\times\mathcal Z\). For any fixed deployed distribution, strong convexity and this bound imply \[\mu\|x-y\|^{2}
 \le\langle\nabla f(x;\bar\theta)-\nabla f(y;\bar\theta),x-y\rangle
 \le2G\|x-y\|.\] Thus \(\operatorname{diam}(\mathcal X)\le2G/\mu\) and any two feasible points have squared separation at most \(4G^{2}/\mu^{2}\). A bias diverging as \((\mu-L\beta)^{-2}\) cannot hold uniformly as the margin vanishes with \(G,\mu\) fixed.

The printed construction hides this dependence. Set \(a=b=1,p=1/4,c=1/2\) in Eq. (31), and \(\rho=1-\beta>0\). Its roots are \[\theta_{\rm PS}=-\frac1{4\rho},\qquad
 \theta_\infty=-\frac1{6\rho},\qquad
 |\theta_\infty-\theta_{\rm PS}|^{2}=\frac1{144\rho^{2}}.\] Even on the smallest convex set containing both, the exact product-domain gradient bound is \[G_{\min}=\frac34+\frac1{12\rho}.\] For example, the allowed sample \(z=1-\beta\theta_{\rm PS}\) evaluated at the allowed parameter \(\theta_\infty\) attains this value. Substitution into the stated upper bias term gives \[\frac{8(G-c)^{2}}{\rho^{2}}
 =\frac{(1+3\rho)^{2}}{18\rho^{4}},\qquad
 \frac{\text{actual bias}}{\text{upper term}}
 =\frac{\rho^{2}}{8(1+3\rho)^{2}}\longrightarrow0.\] The construction therefore does not establish the advertised matching dependence when its other parameters are accounted for.

A direct repair is available. Write \(k=\sqrt{2/\pi}/\sigma\) and interpret the unbiasedness and independence assumptions conditionally on the iterate. For \(|g_i|\le c\), \[\left|\mathbb E[\operatorname{sign}(g_i+N)]-kg_i\right|
\le \frac{k|g_i|^{3}}{6\sigma^{2}},\qquad N\sim N(0,\sigma^{2}).\] This follows by integrating \(1-e^{-u^{2}/2}\le u^{2}/2\), without an asymptotic Taylor remainder. If \(A_t\) is the average of the transmitted bits, then \(\mathbb E[A_t\mid w_t]=k\nabla F(w_t)+e_t\) with \(\|e_t\|_2^{2}\le dk^2c^{6}/(36\sigma^{4})\) and \(\|A_t\|_2^{2}\le d\). Smoothness and Young’s inequality yield \[
\frac1T\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb E\|\nabla F(w_t)\|_2^{2}
\le \frac{2(F(w_0)-F^*)/(\eta T)+Ld\eta}{k}
       +\frac{dc^{6}}{36\sigma^{4}}.\] At the stated learning rate this has the leading terms in Theorem 3, with the missing expectation restored and the dimension dependence made explicit. For independent workers, each coordinate of \(A_t\) has conditional variance at most \(1/M\). Bounding the sign-decision error by its mean absolute deviation similarly gives for Scheme II \[
\frac1T\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb E\|\nabla F(w_t)\|_1
\le\frac{F(w_0)-F^*}{\eta T}+\frac{Ld\eta}{2}
       +\frac{\sigma d\sqrt{2\pi}}{\sqrt M}+\frac{dc^{3}}{3\sigma^{2}}.\] This argument avoids the variance-notation inconsistency between Assumption 3, which bounds variance by \(\sigma_{L,i}\), and Equation (80), which takes a square root of \(\sigma_{L,i}^{2}\). A proof following the latter route instead must distinguish variance from standard deviation. These repairs show why the omission is classified Minor rather than treated as a refutation of expected convergence.

Severity: Minor. Repair verified for the differential identity on a common smooth test domain; full theorem constants are not certified. For \(\beta=0\), set \(S=\alpha t^3f\) and \(\phi=e^{iS}\Psi\). Conjugation eliminates the gradient vector potential and gives \[i\partial_t\phi=\left[-\frac{\Delta}{2t^{3}}
             +(t^{3}+\omega t^{2})f\right]\phi,
 \qquad\omega=\gamma-3\alpha.\] The Lyapunov operator becomes \(O=\tfrac12\sum_j(t^{-2}p_j+2x_j)^{2}+(t^{2}+\omega t)f\). Using \(i[p_j,f]=\partial_jf\) gives the exact multiplier \[\begin{aligned}
 \partial_tO+i[H,O]
 &= (2t+\omega)f-2(t+\omega)x^\top\nabla f\\
 &=2(t+\omega)(f-x^\top\nabla f)-\omega f.\end{aligned}\] Equation (35), page 16, instead has \((2t+\omega)(f-x^\top\nabla f)-2\omega f\). For \(d=1,f=x^{4}/4,t=\omega=x=1\), the correct and printed values are \(-3.25\) and \(-2.75\). A symbolic differential-operator calculation on an arbitrary test function verifies the corrected expression, including all derivative terms. The discrepancy is not an OCR artifact: the page was visually checked. The exponent \(t^{2}\) in the denominator of (34) also differs from \(t^{1/2}\) in (33).

